1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,155
Part 2 starts with the lecture's loop.

2
00:00:02,475 --> 00:00:04,459
Developers commit to the codebase.

3
00:00:04,779 --> 00:00:07,723
It is built and tested: that's the CI half.

4
00:00:08,043 --> 00:00:11,285
Then it is deployed and monitored:
the CD half.

5
00:00:11,605 --> 00:00:14,080
Every stage sends notifications back.

6
00:00:14,317 --> 00:00:18,535
The version in the review adds one box
between test and deploy:

7
00:00:18,535 --> 00:00:23,021
release, the moment a tested version
is handed over for deployment.

8
00:00:23,267 --> 00:00:26,616
Continuous integration
runs scripts on every push.

9
00:00:26,936 --> 00:00:32,465
Many developers share one repository,
and every change passes automated tests,

10
00:00:32,465 --> 00:00:38,136
coding standards, and static analysis,
which finds defects without running code.

11
00:00:38,456 --> 00:00:41,293
You built this in lectures 9 and 21.

12
00:00:41,533 --> 00:00:43,389
Then delivery and deployment.

13
00:00:43,709 --> 00:00:47,239
Continuous delivery keeps every change ready
to release,

14
00:00:47,239 --> 00:00:49,256
but a person presses the button.

15
00:00:49,576 --> 00:00:53,501
Continuous deployment ships
every passing change by itself.

16
00:00:54,233 --> 00:00:58,873
For machine learning, CI also checks
data quality and model performance,

17
00:00:58,873 --> 00:01:00,484
which makes it expensive.

18
00:01:00,804 --> 00:01:05,220
And CD releases a model only
when its metrics are within a tolerance,

19
00:01:05,220 --> 00:01:07,140
and must cope with data drift.

20
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:11,961
This repo deploys an app, not a model;
lecture 15 was the model.

21
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:13,821
Now this repo on the loop.

22
00:01:14,141 --> 00:01:16,695
Build, release and deploy light up;

23
00:01:16,695 --> 00:01:20,051
release is when the tagged images
land in ECR.

24
00:01:20,371 --> 00:01:21,627
Test stays dashed:

25
00:01:21,627 --> 00:01:26,637
the workflow is named continuous integration
and continuous deployment,

26
00:01:26,637 --> 00:01:27,837
but runs no test.

27
00:01:28,157 --> 00:01:29,523
It's the CD half.

28
00:01:29,843 --> 00:01:33,725
And monitor is the color of the run,
plus your own check of the site.

29
00:01:33,967 --> 00:01:39,241
The lecture names several CI/CD services;
the course uses GitHub Actions,

30
00:01:39,241 --> 00:01:44,442
to build the deployment container
and run it, on every commit that asks.

31
00:01:44,762 --> 00:01:46,642
The workflow file stays thin;

32
00:01:46,642 --> 00:01:49,689
the deploy logic
is the playbooks you just ran.

33
00:01:51,133 --> 00:01:55,729
The workflow file, ci_cd_aws.yml,
isn't in the README,

34
00:01:55,729 --> 00:01:59,048
so every step on this tracker is extra.

35
00:01:59,368 --> 00:02:01,664
It starts on every push to main,

36
00:02:01,664 --> 00:02:05,896
but the job runs
only if the commit message contains /run-.

37
00:02:06,133 --> 00:02:12,356
And the last step, Run Deployment Container,
has its own condition on the same message:

38
00:02:12,356 --> 00:02:14,645
it must contain /run-deploy-app.

39
00:02:14,883 --> 00:02:16,504
Two sieves, one sentence.

40
00:02:16,824 --> 00:02:22,392
The message update readme has no /run-,
so the job is skipped and nothing runs.

41
00:02:22,716 --> 00:02:23,716
Your turn.

42
00:02:23,996 --> 00:02:27,153
You push fix typo /run-tests to main.

43
00:02:27,473 --> 00:02:30,503
Nothing, build and push only,
or a full deploy?

44
00:02:39,350 --> 00:02:40,651
Build and push only.

45
00:02:40,971 --> 00:02:45,165
It passes the first sieve,
so the runner builds three new images

46
00:02:45,165 --> 00:02:48,310
and pushes them to ECR,
where they cost storage.

47
00:02:48,630 --> 00:02:52,214
It stops at the second sieve,
so the server keeps the old tag.

48
00:02:52,450 --> 00:02:55,079
/run-deploy-app passes both sieves:

49
00:02:55,079 --> 00:02:59,511
new images, and the containers
on the server are recreated.

50
00:02:59,916 --> 00:03:01,559
And the commit from the opening?

51
00:03:01,879 --> 00:03:03,479
Its slash is a backslash.

52
00:03:03,799 --> 00:03:08,023
The text /run- isn't in it,
so it bounces off the first sieve.

53
00:03:08,343 --> 00:03:09,815
That's the one character.

54
00:03:10,050 --> 00:03:12,393
The tutorial slide says the same:

55
00:03:12,393 --> 00:03:17,645
a workflow file in .github/workflows,
and a keyword in the commit message.

56
00:03:17,965 --> 00:03:22,295
Its keyword, /deploy-app,
belongs to the Google Cloud repo.

57
00:03:22,615 --> 00:03:24,428
Here it's /run-deploy-app.

58
00:03:26,966 --> 00:03:29,715
head_commit means the last commit of a push,

59
00:03:29,715 --> 00:03:33,025
so three commits are judged
by the last message only.

60
00:03:33,345 --> 00:03:34,966
And the match ignores case.

61
00:03:36,117 --> 00:03:39,104
Part 2 needs your own GitHub repository.

62
00:03:39,424 --> 00:03:43,968
Pause, create an empty private one,
point origin at it, and push main.

63
00:03:44,288 --> 00:03:48,298
Its last message has no /run-,
so nothing runs yet.

64
00:03:48,534 --> 00:03:50,787
First, give GitHub your secrets:

65
00:03:50,787 --> 00:03:55,083
in your repository,
Settings, Secrets and variables, Actions.

66
00:03:55,403 --> 00:03:59,109
Add four: the access key ID, the secret key,

67
00:03:59,109 --> 00:04:03,574
your account ID, and AWS_SSH_KEY,
the whole pem file.

68
00:04:03,894 --> 00:04:05,622
The names come from the workflow.

69
00:04:05,867 --> 00:04:10,774
Your badge has a third copy now,
in a vault on GitHub, and your key a second.

70
00:04:11,094 --> 00:04:13,611
Every copy is one more place to protect.

71
00:04:13,850 --> 00:04:16,175
The runner is a fresh Ubuntu machine.

72
00:04:16,495 --> 00:04:21,018
An action from AWS sets up its credentials
from two secrets.

73
00:04:21,338 --> 00:04:27,184
Then it checks who it is, lists S3,
and logs Docker in to your ECR registry.

74
00:04:27,434 --> 00:04:31,167
Then it builds the same toolbox,
from the committed Dockerfile.

75
00:04:31,487 --> 00:04:34,623
So the Helm fix
from part 1 belongs in your commit.

76
00:04:34,943 --> 00:04:38,506
We didn't test whether runners
hit the same certificate error.

77
00:04:38,750 --> 00:04:40,990
Next, a fresh tag from the date,

78
00:04:40,990 --> 00:04:45,470
written to .docker-tag
and to a file GitHub keeps between steps.

79
00:04:45,790 --> 00:04:48,073
Run by hand, it gives a timestamp.

80
00:04:48,684 --> 00:04:53,045
Then plain docker build,
docker tag and docker push for the frontend,

81
00:04:53,045 --> 00:04:55,447
and the same for the other two images.

82
00:04:55,767 --> 00:04:59,628
In CI, the runner builds the app images,
not the playbook.

83
00:04:59,867 --> 00:05:01,787
So the toolbox only deploys.

84
00:05:02,107 --> 00:05:05,584
Compared with your laptop:
same toolbox, same socket.

85
00:05:05,904 --> 00:05:09,972
But the deployment folder sits
at /deployment,

86
00:05:09,972 --> 00:05:13,776
the keys at /root/.ssh, and no app folders.

87
00:05:15,617 --> 00:05:17,345
Last, the SSH key.

88
00:05:17,665 --> 00:05:21,235
The step writes an SSH config,

89
00:05:21,235 --> 00:05:26,710
and your key as ac215_2024.pem,
with mode 600.

90
00:05:27,030 --> 00:05:31,852
It copies both into the deployment folder,
on the runner only, never committed.

91
00:05:36,133 --> 00:05:40,375
The deploy step runs the toolbox
on the runner, with the socket,

92
00:05:40,375 --> 00:05:44,816
two folders, and five environment variables,
DOCKER_TAG among them.

93
00:05:45,050 --> 00:05:47,016
Inside, one long command:

94
00:05:47,016 --> 00:05:49,484
fix permissions, log in to ECR,

95
00:05:49,484 --> 00:05:53,068
run deploy-setup-containers
with the new tag,

96
00:05:53,068 --> 00:05:55,269
then deploy-setup-webserver.

97
00:05:55,589 --> 00:05:58,660
Each link runs only if the one
before succeeded.

98
00:05:58,900 --> 00:06:03,328
Run by hand, as an extra,
it prints the tag and lists the key,

99
00:06:03,328 --> 00:06:07,113
then stops at the ECR login
with our placeholder key.

100
00:06:07,433 --> 00:06:08,820
Neither playbook runs.

101
00:06:10,366 --> 00:06:11,646
Follow one tag.

102
00:06:11,966 --> 00:06:13,673
The date stamps it on the runner.

103
00:06:13,993 --> 00:06:19,007
It reaches Ansible as an extra variable,
which beats anything the playbook sets,

104
00:06:19,007 --> 00:06:22,078
so the server pulls the three images
at that tag.

105
00:06:22,316 --> 00:06:25,633
Then each container is recreated:
the old one stops,

106
00:06:25,633 --> 00:06:28,503
the new one starts,
with a short gap between.

107
00:06:28,750 --> 00:06:33,805
The slides deploy to a Kubernetes cluster,
where, as in lecture 22,

108
00:06:33,805 --> 00:06:38,030
a rolling update starts new pods
before the old ones go.

109
00:06:38,350 --> 00:06:41,059
One machine can only swap; it doesn't roll.

110
00:06:41,300 --> 00:06:42,516
Commit four things.

111
00:06:42,836 --> 00:06:47,167
Your server address on line 30:
CI deploys whatever is committed.

112
00:06:47,487 --> 00:06:50,964
Step 9 undone: line 4 on, line 7 off.

113
00:06:51,284 --> 00:06:52,436
The Helm fix.

114
00:06:52,756 --> 00:06:53,823
The Chroma pin.

115
00:06:54,143 --> 00:06:58,437
git add . also commits your account
ID and NEXTAUTH_SECRET,

116
00:06:58,437 --> 00:07:00,692
so keep the repository private.

117
00:07:00,933 --> 00:07:05,307
Then the slide's three commands,
with this repo's keyword: git add .,

118
00:07:05,307 --> 00:07:09,616
git commit -m "…" with /run-deploy-app
in the message, and git push.

119
00:07:09,936 --> 00:07:11,088
Pause, and push.

120
00:07:16,516 --> 00:07:21,529
A green run means every step that ran exited
with zero, not that the app works.

121
00:07:21,849 --> 00:07:22,889
Reload your site,

122
00:07:22,889 --> 00:07:25,946
and find the new tag
in the server's image column.

123
00:07:26,183 --> 00:07:27,813
In the lecture's words:

124
00:07:27,813 --> 00:07:31,072
is typing
/run-deploy-app continuous delivery,

125
00:07:31,072 --> 00:07:32,818
or continuous deployment?

126
00:07:33,138 --> 00:07:34,610
What would make it the other?

127
00:07:45,450 --> 00:07:46,450
Delivery.

128
00:07:46,687 --> 00:07:49,974
A person decides each release
by typing the keyword,

129
00:07:49,974 --> 00:07:51,743
and nothing is tested first.

130
00:07:52,063 --> 00:07:56,379
Deploy every merge to main,
behind the tests from lecture 21,

131
00:07:56,379 --> 00:07:58,997
and it becomes continuous deployment.

132
00:07:59,317 --> 00:08:00,852
That is Milestone 5.

133
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,008
Back to the README,
for its last step: delete.

134
00:08:05,328 --> 00:08:09,019
It runs the same playbook
with cluster_state=absent.

135
00:08:09,339 --> 00:08:10,683
Look at the when: lines.

136
00:08:11,003 --> 00:08:12,709
Which of these three tasks run?

137
00:08:21,550 --> 00:08:23,214
Run it exactly as written.

138
00:08:23,534 --> 00:08:25,155
Security group, skipping.

139
00:08:25,475 --> 00:08:27,353
EBS volume, skipping.

140
00:08:27,673 --> 00:08:32,793
Then the instance task has no when:
line, and fails: no attribute group_id.

141
00:08:33,113 --> 00:08:35,481
That is before any AWS call.

142
00:08:35,717 --> 00:08:38,407
The skipped task never set
the security group,

143
00:08:38,407 --> 00:08:40,453
so the instance task trips over it.

144
00:08:40,773 --> 00:08:44,129
Even past that,
the removal needs a volume ID

145
00:08:44,129 --> 00:08:46,591
that only a create run registers,

146
00:08:46,591 --> 00:08:49,242
and no task terminates the instance.

147
00:08:49,562 --> 00:08:51,013
So clean up by hand.

148
00:08:51,250 --> 00:08:56,930
In the EC2 console, terminate the instance,
then delete the 50 gigabyte volume,

149
00:08:56,930 --> 00:09:00,957
which outlives it,
the security group and your key pair.

150
00:09:01,277 --> 00:09:03,849
Cost is one of the lecture's challenges

151
00:09:03,849 --> 00:09:07,015
for CD,
and this server bills until you do this.

152
00:09:07,250 --> 00:09:10,258
In ECR, delete the three repositories.

153
00:09:10,578 --> 00:09:13,763
In IAM,
deactivate and delete the access key,

154
00:09:13,763 --> 00:09:17,938
and the user and the policy
if you made them only for this.

155
00:09:18,258 --> 00:09:20,583
On GitHub, delete the four secrets.

156
00:09:20,833 --> 00:09:23,424
On your laptop, remove the toolbox image,

157
00:09:23,424 --> 00:09:27,468
two and a half gigabytes,
and keep or restore your edited files.

158
00:09:32,983 --> 00:09:35,596
The lecture ends
with a review of the course,

159
00:09:35,596 --> 00:09:37,164
on the server you deployed.

160
00:09:37,400 --> 00:09:40,792
Virtual machines and containers,
lectures 1 and 2.

161
00:09:41,112 --> 00:09:43,053
A shared bucket, lecture 3.

162
00:09:43,373 --> 00:09:46,484
And the images the senior
developer pushed by hand:

163
00:09:46,484 --> 00:09:48,130
the runner pushes them now.

164
00:09:48,366 --> 00:09:52,651
The solution architecture:
frontend and API execute;

165
00:09:52,651 --> 00:09:54,958
ECR and the disk hold state.

166
00:09:55,278 --> 00:10:00,633
Retrieval, lecture 5, is the loader
and Chroma; agents, lectures 6 and 7.

167
00:10:00,953 --> 00:10:03,520
Labeling and fine tuning aren't here:

168
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:06,850
the API calls ready-made models
through Bedrock,

169
00:10:06,850 --> 00:10:10,596
plus the cheese classifier trained earlier,
as a file.

170
00:10:10,833 --> 00:10:15,761
Training and pipelines, lectures 13 and 15,
sit beside this server.

171
00:10:16,081 --> 00:10:19,622
The API and frontend containers, lecture 18.

172
00:10:19,942 --> 00:10:22,822
The CI loop, lectures 9 and 21.

173
00:10:23,142 --> 00:10:26,940
Routing, lectures 20 and 22: today's nginx.

174
00:10:27,183 --> 00:10:28,183
Where next?

175
00:10:28,420 --> 00:10:33,092
Put the tests from lecture 21 in front,
and deploy every merge to main.

176
00:10:33,412 --> 00:10:38,511
Replace the copied keys:
a role for the server, and OIDC for GitHub.

177
00:10:38,831 --> 00:10:40,516
And add HTTPS.

178
00:10:41,366 --> 00:10:42,625
Once more, the path.

179
00:10:42,945 --> 00:10:44,694
Two secrets on your laptop.

180
00:10:45,014 --> 00:10:47,403
A toolbox using your laptop's Docker.

181
00:10:47,723 --> 00:10:51,307
Plays on localhost,
or on the server over SSH.

182
00:10:51,627 --> 00:10:55,297
One tag, from the date command
to a recreated container.

183
00:10:55,617 --> 00:10:57,665
One sentence that presses release.

184
00:10:57,985 --> 00:10:59,393
And clean up by hand.

185
00:11:00,233 --> 00:11:01,342
Test yourself.

186
00:11:01,662 --> 00:11:07,166
One: name the two secrets, what each opens,
and three places the access key ends up.

187
00:11:07,486 --> 00:11:09,876
What would a role and OIDC remove?

188
00:11:10,116 --> 00:11:13,926
Two:
why does sh docker-shell.sh fail on Ubuntu

189
00:11:13,926 --> 00:11:15,385
but work on a Mac?

190
00:11:15,705 --> 00:11:18,628
And what did that unquoted pip install
actually do?

191
00:11:18,866 --> 00:11:21,967
Three:
where do the tasks of a localhost play run,

192
00:11:21,967 --> 00:11:23,517
and of an appserver play?

193
00:11:23,837 --> 00:11:26,738
And why can the toolbox
fill the disk of your laptop?

194
00:11:26,983 --> 00:11:31,591
Four: follow one timestamp from the date
command to a running container.

195
00:11:31,911 --> 00:11:34,940
How does the server choose
between the variable and the file?

196
00:11:35,183 --> 00:11:37,103
Five: which of these deploy?

197
00:11:37,423 --> 00:11:40,623
Update readme, fix typo /run-tests,

198
00:11:40,623 --> 00:11:45,103
/RUN-DEPLOY-APP in capitals,
and \run-deploy-app.

199
00:11:45,423 --> 00:11:47,962
And is this pipeline delivery or deployment?

200
00:11:48,282 --> 00:11:50,330
Answers are in the exercises.

